## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design practical, data-safe degraded mode in presence of external dependency failures, with observable signals and measurable rollback.

## Key Concepts

- Degraded mode design and safety nets
- Observability signal fusion (metrics, traces, logs)
- Idempotency and transaction safety
- Canary rollout and rollback criteria
- Postmortem and continuous improvement

## Code Example

```javascript
// Idempotent payment handler for degraded mode
async function processPayment(payload) {
  const key = payload.idempotencyKey;
  const cached = await redis.get(key);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);
  const res = await gateway.charge(payload);
  await redis.set(key, JSON.stringify(res), 'EX', 3600);
  return res;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the degraded path in staging?
- How do you ensure no double-charging when the gateway recovers?

## Why This Is Asked

This evaluates a candidate's ability to design resilient, multi-region metrics pipelines that can withstand regional failures while maintaining data integrity and SLAs.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-region ingestion architecture with blast radius containment
- Per-tenant quotas, token-bucket throttling, and bounded local buffering
- Idempotent writes with deterministic IDs and backpressure signaling
- Circuit breakers and automated regional failover mechanisms
- Observability metrics: ingress latency, drop rate, max replay delay
- Adaptive sampling for surge handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// pseudo-code for idempotent write with backpressure
function writeWithBackpressure(metric) {
  const id = generateDeterministicId(metric);
  if (isBackpressureActive()) {
    bufferLocally(metric);
    return;
  }
  return idempotentWrite(id, metric);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This probes practical fairness under backpressure, observability, and controlled rollout in production.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant fairness under burst
- Backpressure and throttling strategies
- Canary rollouts and rollback criteria
- Observability: per-tenant latency, backlog, saturation
- Budget-aware incident response

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(ratePerSec, capacity){
    this.rate = ratePerSec;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryConsume(n=1){
    const now = Date.now();
    const delta = (now - this.last)/1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + delta * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n){ this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate throttle fairness in a staging canary before rollout?
- What metrics would you collect for a postmortem and how would you communicate budget impact?

